window.__programmeIndexChunk("passages",{"add7d54c132295d2084e22283bb274ca2503b2664dd2c9fc456276f52afbaddd":{"markdown":"## VD-4. The second negative dual action and every sign\n\nFor \\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\), multiplication by \\(z^{-m}\\) on \\(\\mathcal L\\) defines a unitary \\(W_m\\). Since \\(\\Pi(c)\\) is a multiplication field, direct substitution gives an action\n\n<a id=\"equation-vd30\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\beta_m=\\operatorname{Ad}(W_m)|_B,\\qquad\n\\beta_m(\\Pi(c))=\\Pi(c),\\qquad\n\\beta_m(\\ell(w))=w^{-m}\\ell(w).\n\\tag{VD30}\n\\]\nThe inverse is \\(W_{-m}\\), so this is a normal discrete action on the actual generated algebra. Multiplication by \\(z^{-m}\\) changes the Fourier index from \\(k\\) to \\(k-m\\) while leaving \\(n\\) unchanged. Under \\(r=n-k\\) it increases \\(r\\) by \\(m\\). Precisely,\n\n<a id=\"equation-vd31\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\Psi W_m\\Psi^*=S_r^m\\otimes S^{-m},\\qquad\n(S_r\\eta)_r=\\eta_{r-1}.\n\\tag{VD31}\n\\]\nConsequently\n\n<a id=\"equation-vd32\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\beta_m(\\iota(a))&=\\iota(\\alpha^m(a)),&\n\\beta_m(f_{ij})&=f_{i-m,j-m},\\\\\n\\Phi\\beta_m\\Phi^{-1}\n&=\\alpha^m\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}(S^{-m}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{VD32}\n\\]\nThe last equality is an equality of normal automorphisms on the full tensor algebra: it holds on the coefficients and all matrix units, and the uniformly bounded finite-corner passage [VD20](OA-FLOW-VD.md#equation-vd20) proves it everywhere. The right-hand action can also be constructed directly on the complete matrix model by applying \\(\\alpha^m\\) to each entry and shifting both indices, using [VD22](OA-FLOW-VD.md#equation-vd22)'s proof.\n\nAs a sign check, [VD32](OA-FLOW-VD.md#equation-vd32) sends the diagonal in [VD29](OA-FLOW-VD.md#equation-vd29) to\n\\(\\sum_k\\alpha^{m-k}(a)\\otimes E_{k-m,k-m}\\), which reindexes to the same diagonal. It fixes \\(I\\otimes S\\), and sends \\(I\\otimes d_w\\) to \\(w^{-m}(I\\otimes d_w)\\), exactly as [VD30](OA-FLOW-VD.md#equation-vd30) requires.\n\n<a id=\"vd-full-tensor-trace\"><\/a>\n<a id=\"oa-flow.vd.5\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>VD-4. The second negative dual action and every sign<\/h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(m\\in\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z^{-m}\\)\">\\(z^{-m}\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal L\\)\">\\(\\mathcal L\\)<\/span> defines a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_m\\)\">\\(W_m\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Pi(c)\\)\">\\(\\Pi(c)\\)<\/span> is a multiplication field, direct substitution gives an action<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\beta_m=\\operatorname{Ad}(W_m)|_B,\\qquad\n\\beta_m(\\Pi(c))=\\Pi(c),\\qquad\n\\beta_m(\\ell(w))=w^{-m}\\ell(w).\n\\tag{VD30}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\beta_m=\\operatorname{Ad}(W_m)|_B,\\qquad\n\\beta_m(\\Pi(c))=\\Pi(c),\\qquad\n\\beta_m(\\ell(w))=w^{-m}\\ell(w).\n\\tag{VD30}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe inverse is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W_{-m}\\)\">\\(W_{-m}\\)<\/span>, so this is a normal discrete action on the actual generated algebra. Multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z^{-m}\\)\">\\(z^{-m}\\)<\/span> changes the Fourier index from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k\\)\">\\(k\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k-m\\)\">\\(k-m\\)<\/span> while leaving <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> unchanged. Under <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r=n-k\\)\">\\(r=n-k\\)<\/span> it increases <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span>. Precisely,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\Psi W_m\\Psi^*=S_r^m\\otimes S^{-m},\\qquad\n(S_r\\eta)_r=\\eta_{r-1}.\n\\tag{VD31}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\Psi W_m\\Psi^*=S_r^m\\otimes S^{-m},\\qquad\n(S_r\\eta)_r=\\eta_{r-1}.\n\\tag{VD31}\n\\]<\/span>\nConsequently<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\beta_m(\\iota(a))&amp;=\\iota(\\alpha^m(a)),&amp;\n\\beta_m(f_{ij})&amp;=f_{i-m,j-m},\\\\\n\\Phi\\beta_m\\Phi^{-1}\n&amp;=\\alpha^m\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}(S^{-m}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{VD32}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\beta_m(\\iota(a))&amp;=\\iota(\\alpha^m(a)),&amp;\n\\beta_m(f_{ij})&amp;=f_{i-m,j-m},\\\\\n\\Phi\\beta_m\\Phi^{-1}\n&amp;=\\alpha^m\\bar\\otimes\\operatorname{Ad}(S^{-m}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{VD32}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe last equality is an equality of normal automorphisms on the full tensor algebra: it holds on the coefficients and all matrix units, and the uniformly bounded finite-corner passage <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#equation-vd20\">VD20<\/a> proves it everywhere. The right-hand action can also be constructed directly on the complete matrix model by applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\alpha^m\\)\">\\(\\alpha^m\\)<\/span> to each entry and shifting both indices, using <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#equation-vd22\">VD22<\/a>'s proof.<\/p>\n<p>As a sign check, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#equation-vd32\">VD32<\/a> sends the diagonal in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#equation-vd29\">VD29<\/a> to\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sum_k\\alpha^{m-k}(a)\\otimes E_{k-m,k-m}\\)\">\\(\\sum_k\\alpha^{m-k}(a)\\otimes E_{k-m,k-m}\\)<\/span>, which reindexes to the same diagonal. It fixes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\otimes S\\)\">\\(I\\otimes S\\)<\/span>, and sends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\otimes d_w\\)\">\\(I\\otimes d_w\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(w^{-m}(I\\otimes d_w)\\)\">\\(w^{-m}(I\\otimes d_w)\\)<\/span>, exactly as <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-VD.html#equation-vd30\">VD30<\/a> requires.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-VD.md","start_offset":19877,"end_offset":21702,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"add8b64c77d09744ef2dc7ced92afce86304130542b0c0c50e8d0dcf54a3a050":{"markdown":"## MT-2. A bounded logarithm supplies an already bounded inner derivation\n\nWrite \\(\\pi=\\pi_\\psi\\). [MW-4](OA-FLOW-MW.md#oa-flow.mw.4) gives on the entire algebra\n\n<a id=\"equation-mt8\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma_t^\\psi(x))=e^{itL}\\pi(x)e^{-itL}.\n\\tag{MT8}\n\\]\nThe norm-convergent exponential series and its remainder after the linear term show, uniformly for \\(\\|x\\|\\le1\\),\n\n<a id=\"equation-mt9\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi(\\sigma_t^\\psi(x))-\\pi(x)}{t}\n\\longrightarrow i[L,\\pi(x)]\\qquad(t\\to0)\n\\tag{MT9}\n\\]\nin operator norm. Since \\(\\pi(M)\\) is norm closed, the limit belongs to that algebra. Its isometric inverse defines a bounded complex-linear map \\(d:M\\to M\\) with\n\n<a id=\"equation-mt10\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\n\\pi(d(x))=i[L,\\pi(x)],\\qquad\n\\|d\\|\\le2\\|L\\|,\\qquad\nd(xy)=d(x)y+xd(y),\\qquad d(x^*)=d(x)^*.\n\\tag{MT10}\n\\]\nThe commutator identities directly give the last two assertions. Thus no automatic boundedness result is needed.\n\nFor completeness the norm-differentiable group is its exponential. Differentiating the group law gives \\((\\sigma_t^\\psi)'=\\sigma_t^\\psi d=d\\sigma_t^\\psi\\). The norm derivative of \\(e^{-td}\\sigma_t^\\psi\\), computed by its convergent series and the product rule, is zero. The Banach-valued fundamental theorem in L35 Section 2 makes it constant and equal to \\(I\\) at zero. Therefore \\(\\sigma_t^\\psi=e^{td}\\).\n\nApply the actual already-bounded construction in L35 Sections 2–6 to \\(d\\). Its annihilator projections, norm sums and finite spectral-block transfer, followed by its complete diagonal-localization lemma, give \\(b=b^*\\in M\\) with\n\n<a id=\"equation-mt11\"><\/a>\n\n\\[\nd(x)=i[b,x],\\qquad \\sigma_t^\\psi(x)=e^{itb}xe^{-itb}.\n\\tag{MT11}\n\\]\nFor \\(d=0\\) take \\(b=0\\). For nonzero \\(d\\) that proof actually supplies \\(0\\le b\\le\\|d\\|1\\); we only need bounded selfadjointness. The inner group and \\(\\sigma^\\psi\\) have the same bounded generator and hence agree by the preceding uniqueness argument. Formula ([MT11](OA-FLOW-MT.md#equation-mt11)), applied to \\(b\\), gives \\(\\sigma_t^\\psi(b)=b\\), so \\(b\\) lies in the centralizer \\(M_\\psi\\).\n\n<a id=\"mt-3\"><\/a>\n\n<a id=\"oa-flow.mt.3\"><\/a>","html":"<h2>MT-2. A bounded logarithm supplies an already bounded inner derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi=\\pi_\\psi\\)\">\\(\\pi=\\pi_\\psi\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MW.html#oa-flow.mw.4\">MW-4<\/a> gives on the entire algebra<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma_t^\\psi(x))=e^{itL}\\pi(x)e^{-itL}.\n\\tag{MT8}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma_t^\\psi(x))=e^{itL}\\pi(x)e^{-itL}.\n\\tag{MT8}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe norm-convergent exponential series and its remainder after the linear term show, uniformly for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x\\|\\le1\\)\">\\(\\|x\\|\\le1\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\frac{\\pi(\\sigma_t^\\psi(x))-\\pi(x)}{t}\n\\longrightarrow i[L,\\pi(x)]\\qquad(t\\to0)\n\\tag{MT9}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\frac{\\pi(\\sigma_t^\\psi(x))-\\pi(x)}{t}\n\\longrightarrow i[L,\\pi(x)]\\qquad(t\\to0)\n\\tag{MT9}\n\\]<\/span>\nin operator norm. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\pi(M)\\)\">\\(\\pi(M)\\)<\/span> is norm closed, the limit belongs to that algebra. Its isometric inverse defines a bounded complex-linear map <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d:M\\to M\\)\">\\(d:M\\to M\\)<\/span> with<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\pi(d(x))=i[L,\\pi(x)],\\qquad\n\\|d\\|\\le2\\|L\\|,\\qquad\nd(xy)=d(x)y+xd(y),\\qquad d(x^*)=d(x)^*.\n\\tag{MT10}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\pi(d(x))=i[L,\\pi(x)],\\qquad\n\\|d\\|\\le2\\|L\\|,\\qquad\nd(xy)=d(x)y+xd(y),\\qquad d(x^*)=d(x)^*.\n\\tag{MT10}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe commutator identities directly give the last two assertions. Thus no automatic boundedness result is needed.<\/p>\n<p>For completeness the norm-differentiable group is its exponential. Differentiating the group law gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((\\sigma_t^\\psi)&#x27;=\\sigma_t^\\psi d=d\\sigma_t^\\psi\\)\">\\((\\sigma_t^\\psi)&#x27;=\\sigma_t^\\psi d=d\\sigma_t^\\psi\\)<\/span>. The norm derivative of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-td}\\sigma_t^\\psi\\)\">\\(e^{-td}\\sigma_t^\\psi\\)<\/span>, computed by its convergent series and the product rule, is zero. The Banach-valued fundamental theorem in L35 Section 2 makes it constant and equal to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I\\)\">\\(I\\)<\/span> at zero. Therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi=e^{td}\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi=e^{td}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Apply the actual already-bounded construction in L35 Sections 2–6 to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>. Its annihilator projections, norm sums and finite spectral-block transfer, followed by its complete diagonal-localization lemma, give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=b^*\\in M\\)\">\\(b=b^*\\in M\\)<\/span> with<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nd(x)=i[b,x],\\qquad \\sigma_t^\\psi(x)=e^{itb}xe^{-itb}.\n\\tag{MT11}\n\\]\">\\[\nd(x)=i[b,x],\\qquad \\sigma_t^\\psi(x)=e^{itb}xe^{-itb}.\n\\tag{MT11}\n\\]<\/span>\nFor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d=0\\)\">\\(d=0\\)<\/span> take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b=0\\)\">\\(b=0\\)<\/span>. For nonzero <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span> that proof actually supplies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0\\le b\\le\\|d\\|1\\)\">\\(0\\le b\\le\\|d\\|1\\)<\/span>; we only need bounded selfadjointness. The inner group and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma^\\psi\\)\">\\(\\sigma^\\psi\\)<\/span> have the same bounded generator and hence agree by the preceding uniqueness argument. Formula (<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-MT.html#equation-mt11\">MT11<\/a>), applied to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span>, gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi(b)=b\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^\\psi(b)=b\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b\\)\">\\(b\\)<\/span> lies in the centralizer <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_\\psi\\)\">\\(M_\\psi\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-MT.md","start_offset":5284,"end_offset":7364,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad35d265103a99ebb3a30dab81be2c4da2e21d406a77ac782ac47702b03db24e":{"markdown":"## The norm-closed criterion on an ordinary Banach space\n\nLet \\(E\\) be a complex Banach space and let \\(A:D\\subset E\\to E\\) be linear, norm-closed and norm-densely defined. Then \\(A\\), with exactly the domain \\(D\\), generates a unique strongly continuous group of surjective isometries if and only if\n\\[\n \\|(I-sA)x\\|\\geq\\|x\\|\\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x\\in D),\n \\qquad\n (I-r_+A)D=(I+r_-A)D=E\n \\tag{NB1}\n\\]\nfor some \\(r_+,r_->0\\). The hypotheses here concern norm topology; no dual space or weak-star closedness is required.\n\nWe first obtain every real resolvent directly in norm. If \\(I-sA\\) is onto for \\(s\\ne0\\), its inverse \\(J_s\\) is a contraction by (NB1). Onto parameters form an open set in each half-line: the bounded factorization\n\\[\n (I-rA)J_s=I-(r-s)AJ_s,\\qquad\n AJ_s=(J_s-I)/s\n \\tag{NB2}\n\\]\nand the Neumann series give surjectivity for \\(r\\) near \\(s\\). The lower bound in (NB1) supplies injectivity as well.\n\nFor two such parameters, applying the two inverse identities on \\(D\\) gives\n\\[\n J_r-J_s=(r-s)J_rAJ_s,\\qquad\n \\|J_r-J_s\\|\\leq\\frac{2|r-s|}{|s|}.\n \\tag{NB3}\n\\]\nTo see the algebraic identity, apply \\(I-rA\\) to \\(J_sx\\), then apply \\(J_r\\), and rearrange. The estimate uses \\(\\|J_r\\|,\\|J_s\\|\\leq1\\) and (NB2). Suppose \\(s_n\\to s\\ne0\\) through onto parameters. Equation (NB3) makes \\(J_{s_n}\\) Cauchy in operator norm, since the denominators stay away from zero. Let \\(J\\) be its contraction limit. For every \\(x\\in E\\),\n\\[\n J_{s_n}x\\longrightarrow Jx,\\qquad\n AJ_{s_n}x=\\frac{J_{s_n}x-x}{s_n}\n       \\longrightarrow\\frac{Jx-x}{s}\n \\quad\\text{in norm}.\n \\tag{NB4}\n\\]\nNorm-closedness of \\(A\\) gives \\(Jx\\in D\\) and \\((I-sA)Jx=x\\). Thus the onto-parameter set is also closed in each half-line. The interval connectedness argument given above after (H5), together with the two parameters in (NB1), proves\n\\[\n J_s=(I-sA)^{-1}\\text{ exists and is contractive for every }s\\ne0.\n \\tag{NB5}\n\\]\nThe zero space is included, with its unique linear maps.\n\nFor clarity about applying the construction, (NB5) and the full inverse identities supply all of the inputs to the norm argument (H10)–(H13) in [the semigroup construction](#oa-flow.hy.preadjoint). That argument uses an arbitrary Banach space, a dense domain and the contractive resolvents of both signs. Its construction does not use a predual. With \\(Y=E\\), put \\(A_\\varepsilon=AJ_\\varepsilon=(J_\\varepsilon-I)/\\varepsilon\\), \\(\\varepsilon>0\\). The resolvent identity gives commutation and\n\\[\n J_\\varepsilon x-x=\\varepsilon J_\\varepsilon Ax,\\qquad\n A_\\varepsilon x=J_\\varepsilon Ax\\longrightarrow Ax\n \\quad(x\\in D),\\qquad\n \\|e^{tA_\\varepsilon}\\|\\leq1\\quad(t\\geq0).\n \\tag{NB6}\n\\]\nDensity extends \\(J_\\varepsilon\\to I\\) strongly to all of \\(E\\). The differentiated exponential product in (H11b) gives\n\\(\\sup_{0\\leq t\\leq b}\\|(e^{tA_\\varepsilon}-e^{tA_\\eta})x\\|\n \\leq b\\|(A_\\varepsilon-A_\\eta)x\\|\\) on \\(D\\).\nDensity and contractivity give strongly continuous contraction limits on all of \\(E\\), uniformly on bounded positive time intervals. Passing through the bounded products proves their semigroup law.\n\nThe generator-domain argument (H12a)–(H13) applies with these same inputs. In detail, the integral identity\n\\(T_+(t)x-x=\\int_0^tT_+(u)Ax\\,du\\) for \\(x\\in D\\) makes its right generator \\(G_+\\) extend \\(A\\). If \\(G_+y=\\varepsilon^{-1}y\\), differentiating \\(e^{-t/\\varepsilon}T_+(t)y\\) makes it constant; contractivity forces \\(y=0\\). Thus \\(I-\\varepsilon G_+\\) is injective. For any \\(y\\in D(G_+)\\), the vector \\(q=J_\\varepsilon(I-\\varepsilon G_+)y\\in D\\) has the same image as \\(y\\) under that injective operator. Hence \\(y=q\\in D\\) and \\(G_+=A\\) on its entire domain. The differentiability for positive times used here follows from the semigroup law on the right difference quotients; left difference quotients at a positive time give the same derivative, as shown in (H12a)'s following argument.\n\nApply the construction also to \\(-A\\). Resolvent commutation gives commuting semigroups \\(T_+,T_-\\), with full generators \\(A,-A\\). On \\(D\\), differentiating \\(T_+(t)T_-(t)x\\) gives zero; each semigroup preserves the generator domain and commutes with \\(A\\) there. The initial value is \\(x\\), and density extends the identity to all of \\(E\\). The reversed product is also the identity. The definition \\(T_t=T_+(t)\\) for \\(t\\geq0\\), \\(T_t=T_-(-t)\\) for \\(t<0\\), therefore gives a strongly continuous group. Its operators and their inverses are contractions, hence are isometries. Its two-sided norm generator is exactly \\(A\\).\n\nConversely, [the norm generator theorem](OA-FLOW-L94.md#oa-flow.gen.norm) supplies closedness and density for any such group. Formula [NR6](OA-FLOW-L95.md#oa-flow.res.norm) gives all the contractive inverses \\(J_s\\), so both conditions in (NB1) follow, including the lower bound by applying \\(J_s\\) to \\((I-sA)x\\).\n\nFinally the complete norm integral and moment calculation (H15a)–(H16) in [the Euler argument](#oa-flow.hy.euler) applies directly to this strongly continuous group on \\(E\\), using (NR6). Hence, for every \\(x\\in E\\),\n\\[\n T_tx=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(I-(t/n)A)^{-n}x\n \\quad\\text{in norm, uniformly on compact real time intervals},\n \\tag{NB7}\n\\]\nwhere the \\(t=0\\) factor is \\(I_E\\). Any other group with generator \\(A\\) has the same resolvents by (NR6) and the same Euler limit, proving uniqueness. The norm moment estimate (H19b) likewise gives the directional Yosida limits\n\\[\n T_tx=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}e^{\\,tA(I-\\varepsilon A)^{-1}}x\n       \\quad(t\\geq0),\\qquad\n T_tx=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}e^{\\,tA(I+\\varepsilon A)^{-1}}x\n       \\quad(t\\leq0),\n \\tag{NB8}\n\\]\nuniformly on compact subsets of the indicated half-lines. The bounded-generator formulas and the time-direction distinction are the same as in (H19); norm convergence now holds on the entire ordinary Banach space.\n\nThis is the norm-topology form of the group criterion in Takesaki, *Theory of Operator Algebras II*, XI.1, p.312 and Theorem1.20, pp.326–327. The operator-norm Cauchy argument (NB3)–(NB4) supplies the range-closure step for this setting.","html":"<h2>The norm-closed criterion on an ordinary Banach space<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span> be a complex Banach space and let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A:D\\subset E\\to E\\)\">\\(A:D\\subset E\\to E\\)<\/span> be linear, norm-closed and norm-densely defined. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>, with exactly the domain <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, generates a unique strongly continuous group of surjective isometries if and only if\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|(I-sA)x\\|\\geq\\|x\\|\\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x\\in D),\n \\qquad\n (I-r_+A)D=(I+r_-A)D=E\n \\tag{NB1}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|(I-sA)x\\|\\geq\\|x\\|\\quad(s\\in\\mathbb R,\\ x\\in D),\n \\qquad\n (I-r_+A)D=(I+r_-A)D=E\n \\tag{NB1}\n\\]<\/span>\nfor some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r_+,r_-&gt;0\\)\">\\(r_+,r_-&gt;0\\)<\/span>. The hypotheses here concern norm topology; no dual space or weak-star closedness is required.<\/p>\n<p>We first obtain every real resolvent directly in norm. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I-sA\\)\">\\(I-sA\\)<\/span> is onto for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\ne0\\)\">\\(s\\ne0\\)<\/span>, its inverse <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_s\\)\">\\(J_s\\)<\/span> is a contraction by (NB1). Onto parameters form an open set in each half-line: the bounded factorization\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (I-rA)J_s=I-(r-s)AJ_s,\\qquad\n AJ_s=(J_s-I)/s\n \\tag{NB2}\n\\]\">\\[\n (I-rA)J_s=I-(r-s)AJ_s,\\qquad\n AJ_s=(J_s-I)/s\n \\tag{NB2}\n\\]<\/span>\nand the Neumann series give surjectivity for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r\\)\">\\(r\\)<\/span> near <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s\\)\">\\(s\\)<\/span>. The lower bound in (NB1) supplies injectivity as well.<\/p>\n<p>For two such parameters, applying the two inverse identities on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span> gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J_r-J_s=(r-s)J_rAJ_s,\\qquad\n \\|J_r-J_s\\|\\leq\\frac{2|r-s|}{|s|}.\n \\tag{NB3}\n\\]\">\\[\n J_r-J_s=(r-s)J_rAJ_s,\\qquad\n \\|J_r-J_s\\|\\leq\\frac{2|r-s|}{|s|}.\n \\tag{NB3}\n\\]<\/span>\nTo see the algebraic identity, apply <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I-rA\\)\">\\(I-rA\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_sx\\)\">\\(J_sx\\)<\/span>, then apply <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_r\\)\">\\(J_r\\)<\/span>, and rearrange. The estimate uses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|J_r\\|,\\|J_s\\|\\leq1\\)\">\\(\\|J_r\\|,\\|J_s\\|\\leq1\\)<\/span> and (NB2). Suppose <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s_n\\to s\\ne0\\)\">\\(s_n\\to s\\ne0\\)<\/span> through onto parameters. Equation (NB3) makes <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_{s_n}\\)\">\\(J_{s_n}\\)<\/span> Cauchy in operator norm, since the denominators stay away from zero. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> be its contraction limit. For every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in E\\)\">\\(x\\in E\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J_{s_n}x\\longrightarrow Jx,\\qquad\n AJ_{s_n}x=\\frac{J_{s_n}x-x}{s_n}\n       \\longrightarrow\\frac{Jx-x}{s}\n \\quad\\text{in norm}.\n \\tag{NB4}\n\\]\">\\[\n J_{s_n}x\\longrightarrow Jx,\\qquad\n AJ_{s_n}x=\\frac{J_{s_n}x-x}{s_n}\n       \\longrightarrow\\frac{Jx-x}{s}\n \\quad\\text{in norm}.\n \\tag{NB4}\n\\]<\/span>\nNorm-closedness of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Jx\\in D\\)\">\\(Jx\\in D\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((I-sA)Jx=x\\)\">\\((I-sA)Jx=x\\)<\/span>. Thus the onto-parameter set is also closed in each half-line. The interval connectedness argument given above after (H5), together with the two parameters in (NB1), proves\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J_s=(I-sA)^{-1}\\text{ exists and is contractive for every }s\\ne0.\n \\tag{NB5}\n\\]\">\\[\n J_s=(I-sA)^{-1}\\text{ exists and is contractive for every }s\\ne0.\n \\tag{NB5}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe zero space is included, with its unique linear maps.<\/p>\n<p>For clarity about applying the construction, (NB5) and the full inverse identities supply all of the inputs to the norm argument (H10)–(H13) in <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-flow.hy.preadjoint\">the semigroup construction<\/a>. That argument uses an arbitrary Banach space, a dense domain and the contractive resolvents of both signs. Its construction does not use a predual. With <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y=E\\)\">\\(Y=E\\)<\/span>, put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A_\\varepsilon=AJ_\\varepsilon=(J_\\varepsilon-I)/\\varepsilon\\)\">\\(A_\\varepsilon=AJ_\\varepsilon=(J_\\varepsilon-I)/\\varepsilon\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)\">\\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\)<\/span>. The resolvent identity gives commutation and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n J_\\varepsilon x-x=\\varepsilon J_\\varepsilon Ax,\\qquad\n A_\\varepsilon x=J_\\varepsilon Ax\\longrightarrow Ax\n \\quad(x\\in D),\\qquad\n \\|e^{tA_\\varepsilon}\\|\\leq1\\quad(t\\geq0).\n \\tag{NB6}\n\\]\">\\[\n J_\\varepsilon x-x=\\varepsilon J_\\varepsilon Ax,\\qquad\n A_\\varepsilon x=J_\\varepsilon Ax\\longrightarrow Ax\n \\quad(x\\in D),\\qquad\n \\|e^{tA_\\varepsilon}\\|\\leq1\\quad(t\\geq0).\n \\tag{NB6}\n\\]<\/span>\nDensity extends <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_\\varepsilon\\to I\\)\">\\(J_\\varepsilon\\to I\\)<\/span> strongly to all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>. The differentiated exponential product in (H11b) gives\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sup_{0\\leq t\\leq b}\\|(e^{tA_\\varepsilon}-e^{tA_\\eta})x\\|\n \\leq b\\|(A_\\varepsilon-A_\\eta)x\\|\\)\">\\(\\sup_{0\\leq t\\leq b}\\|(e^{tA_\\varepsilon}-e^{tA_\\eta})x\\|\n \\leq b\\|(A_\\varepsilon-A_\\eta)x\\|\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>.\nDensity and contractivity give strongly continuous contraction limits on all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, uniformly on bounded positive time intervals. Passing through the bounded products proves their semigroup law.<\/p>\n<p>The generator-domain argument (H12a)–(H13) applies with these same inputs. In detail, the integral identity\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_+(t)x-x=\\int_0^tT_+(u)Ax\\,du\\)\">\\(T_+(t)x-x=\\int_0^tT_+(u)Ax\\,du\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in D\\)\">\\(x\\in D\\)<\/span> makes its right generator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G_+\\)\">\\(G_+\\)<\/span> extend <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G_+y=\\varepsilon^{-1}y\\)\">\\(G_+y=\\varepsilon^{-1}y\\)<\/span>, differentiating <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e^{-t/\\varepsilon}T_+(t)y\\)\">\\(e^{-t/\\varepsilon}T_+(t)y\\)<\/span> makes it constant; contractivity forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=0\\)\">\\(y=0\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I-\\varepsilon G_+\\)\">\\(I-\\varepsilon G_+\\)<\/span> is injective. For any <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\in D(G_+)\\)\">\\(y\\in D(G_+)\\)<\/span>, the vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q=J_\\varepsilon(I-\\varepsilon G_+)y\\in D\\)\">\\(q=J_\\varepsilon(I-\\varepsilon G_+)y\\in D\\)<\/span> has the same image as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y\\)\">\\(y\\)<\/span> under that injective operator. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(y=q\\in D\\)\">\\(y=q\\in D\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G_+=A\\)\">\\(G_+=A\\)<\/span> on its entire domain. The differentiability for positive times used here follows from the semigroup law on the right difference quotients; left difference quotients at a positive time give the same derivative, as shown in (H12a)'s following argument.<\/p>\n<p>Apply the construction also to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(-A\\)\">\\(-A\\)<\/span>. Resolvent commutation gives commuting semigroups <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_+,T_-\\)\">\\(T_+,T_-\\)<\/span>, with full generators <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A,-A\\)\">\\(A,-A\\)<\/span>. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>, differentiating <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_+(t)T_-(t)x\\)\">\\(T_+(t)T_-(t)x\\)<\/span> gives zero; each semigroup preserves the generator domain and commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> there. The initial value is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, and density extends the identity to all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>. The reversed product is also the identity. The definition <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_t=T_+(t)\\)\">\\(T_t=T_+(t)\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t\\geq0\\)\">\\(t\\geq0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_t=T_-(-t)\\)\">\\(T_t=T_-(-t)\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t&lt;0\\)\">\\(t&lt;0\\)<\/span>, therefore gives a strongly continuous group. Its operators and their inverses are contractions, hence are isometries. Its two-sided norm generator is exactly <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L94.html#oa-flow.gen.norm\">the norm generator theorem<\/a> supplies closedness and density for any such group. Formula <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L95.html#oa-flow.res.norm\">NR6<\/a> gives all the contractive inverses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_s\\)\">\\(J_s\\)<\/span>, so both conditions in (NB1) follow, including the lower bound by applying <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J_s\\)\">\\(J_s\\)<\/span> to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((I-sA)x\\)\">\\((I-sA)x\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Finally the complete norm integral and moment calculation (H15a)–(H16) in <a title=\"Reference location unresolved in this edition\" data-unresolved-reference=\"#oa-flow.hy.euler\">the Euler argument<\/a> applies directly to this strongly continuous group on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, using (NR6). Hence, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in E\\)\">\\(x\\in E\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_tx=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(I-(t/n)A)^{-n}x\n \\quad\\text{in norm, uniformly on compact real time intervals},\n \\tag{NB7}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_tx=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}(I-(t/n)A)^{-n}x\n \\quad\\text{in norm, uniformly on compact real time intervals},\n \\tag{NB7}\n\\]<\/span>\nwhere the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(t=0\\)\">\\(t=0\\)<\/span> factor is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(I_E\\)\">\\(I_E\\)<\/span>. Any other group with generator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(A\\)\">\\(A\\)<\/span> has the same resolvents by (NR6) and the same Euler limit, proving uniqueness. The norm moment estimate (H19b) likewise gives the directional Yosida limits\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n T_tx=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}e^{\\,tA(I-\\varepsilon A)^{-1}}x\n       \\quad(t\\geq0),\\qquad\n T_tx=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}e^{\\,tA(I+\\varepsilon A)^{-1}}x\n       \\quad(t\\leq0),\n \\tag{NB8}\n\\]\">\\[\n T_tx=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}e^{\\,tA(I-\\varepsilon A)^{-1}}x\n       \\quad(t\\geq0),\\qquad\n T_tx=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}e^{\\,tA(I+\\varepsilon A)^{-1}}x\n       \\quad(t\\leq0),\n \\tag{NB8}\n\\]<\/span>\nuniformly on compact subsets of the indicated half-lines. The bounded-generator formulas and the time-direction distinction are the same as in (H19); norm convergence now holds on the entire ordinary Banach space.<\/p>\n<p>This is the norm-topology form of the group criterion in Takesaki, <em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em>, XI.1, p.312 and Theorem1.20, pp.326–327. The operator-norm Cauchy argument (NB3)–(NB4) supplies the range-closure step for this setting.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L96.md","start_offset":26586,"end_offset":32611,"role":"complete_registered_reader_section"},"ad51c2b21a194af60a3f3ce9cc3093ce96d34c47f709ff3d0dc0b83e982420ab":{"markdown":"<a id=\"wr-models\"><\/a>\n\n## Finite clocks and infinite translations\n\nFor \\(G=\\mathbb Z/5\\mathbb Z\\) with counting measure, let \\(H=\\mathbb C^5\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\), with basis vectors \\(\\delta_r\\) in the first factor. Put\n\n$$U_s(\\delta_r\\otimes e)=\\delta_{r+s}\\otimes e,\\qquad\nV_k(\\delta_r\\otimes e)=e^{-2\\pi i kr/5}\\delta_r\\otimes e.$$\n\nThe character is \\(\\chi_k(s)=e^{2\\pi i ks/5}\\), and the pair obeys (R1). Its compact-kernel operator is\n\n<a id=\"equation-r22\"><\/a>\n\n$$T_{\\delta_r,\\delta_v}=\\pi(1_{\\{r\\}})U_{r-v}\n=\\theta_{\\delta_r,\\delta_v}\\otimes1_{\\mathbb C^2}. \\tag{R22}$$\n\nThus \\(P_0\\) for \\(e_0=\\delta_0\\) has a two-dimensional range. The joint generated algebra is \\(M_5(\\mathbb C)\\otimes1\\), with commutant \\(1\\otimes M_2(\\mathbb C)\\).\n\nFor \\(G=\\mathbb Z\\), the dual group is the circle. On \\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)\\),\n\n$$U_s\\delta_n=\\delta_{n+s},\\qquad V_z\\delta_n=z^{-n}\\delta_n.$$\n\nThis pair is irreducible by the theorem. There is no nonzero finite-dimensional Weyl pair for these full groups: on a \\(d\\)-dimensional space the relation for \\(s=1\\) would give\n\\(\\det(U_1V_zU_1^*)=\\det(zV_z)=z^d\\det(V_z)\\), so \\(z^d=1\\) for every \\(z\\in\\mathbb T\\), impossible when \\(d>0\\).\n\nFor \\(G=\\mathbb R\\), the normalized tent\n\n$$e_a(r)=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2a}}\\left(1-\\frac{|r|}{a}\\right)_+\n\\qquad(a>0)$$\n\nhas \\(L^2\\) norm one. In a canonical pair with multiplicity \\(E\\), the operator \\(T_{e_a,e_a}\\) projects onto \\(e_a\\otimes E\\). Changing \\(a\\) changes the particular range subspace while preserving its Hilbert dimension.","html":"<h2>Finite clocks and infinite translations<\/h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\mathbb Z/5\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(G=\\mathbb Z/5\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span> with counting measure, let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H=\\mathbb C^5\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\)\">\\(H=\\mathbb C^5\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\)<\/span>, with basis vectors <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\delta_r\\)\">\\(\\delta_r\\)<\/span> in the first factor. Put<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[U_s(\\delta_r\\otimes e)=\\delta_{r+s}\\otimes e,\\qquad\nV_k(\\delta_r\\otimes e)=e^{-2\\pi i kr/5}\\delta_r\\otimes e.\\]\">\\[U_s(\\delta_r\\otimes e)=\\delta_{r+s}\\otimes e,\\qquad\nV_k(\\delta_r\\otimes e)=e^{-2\\pi i kr/5}\\delta_r\\otimes e.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The character is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\chi_k(s)=e^{2\\pi i ks/5}\\)\">\\(\\chi_k(s)=e^{2\\pi i ks/5}\\)<\/span>, and the pair obeys (R1). Its compact-kernel operator is<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[T_{\\delta_r,\\delta_v}=\\pi(1_{\\{r\\}})U_{r-v}\n=\\theta_{\\delta_r,\\delta_v}\\otimes1_{\\mathbb C^2}. \\tag{R22}\\]\">\\[T_{\\delta_r,\\delta_v}=\\pi(1_{\\{r\\}})U_{r-v}\n=\\theta_{\\delta_r,\\delta_v}\\otimes1_{\\mathbb C^2}. \\tag{R22}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_0\\)\">\\(P_0\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_0=\\delta_0\\)\">\\(e_0=\\delta_0\\)<\/span> has a two-dimensional range. The joint generated algebra is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_5(\\mathbb C)\\otimes1\\)\">\\(M_5(\\mathbb C)\\otimes1\\)<\/span>, with commutant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\otimes M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)\">\\(1\\otimes M_2(\\mathbb C)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(G=\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>, the dual group is the circle. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)\\)\">\\(\\ell^2(\\mathbb Z)\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[U_s\\delta_n=\\delta_{n+s},\\qquad V_z\\delta_n=z^{-n}\\delta_n.\\]\">\\[U_s\\delta_n=\\delta_{n+s},\\qquad V_z\\delta_n=z^{-n}\\delta_n.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>This pair is irreducible by the theorem. There is no nonzero finite-dimensional Weyl pair for these full groups: on a <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\)\">\\(d\\)<\/span>-dimensional space the relation for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s=1\\)\">\\(s=1\\)<\/span> would give\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\det(U_1V_zU_1^*)=\\det(zV_z)=z^d\\det(V_z)\\)\">\\(\\det(U_1V_zU_1^*)=\\det(zV_z)=z^d\\det(V_z)\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z^d=1\\)\">\\(z^d=1\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\in\\mathbb T\\)\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb T\\)<\/span>, impossible when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d&gt;0\\)\">\\(d&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G=\\mathbb R\\)\">\\(G=\\mathbb R\\)<\/span>, the normalized tent<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[e_a(r)=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2a}}\\left(1-\\frac{|r|}{a}\\right)_+\n\\qquad(a&gt;0)\\]\">\\[e_a(r)=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2a}}\\left(1-\\frac{|r|}{a}\\right)_+\n\\qquad(a&gt;0)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>has <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(L^2\\)\">\\(L^2\\)<\/span> norm one. In a canonical pair with multiplicity <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(E\\)\">\\(E\\)<\/span>, the operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_{e_a,e_a}\\)\">\\(T_{e_a,e_a}\\)<\/span> projects onto <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(e_a\\otimes E\\)\">\\(e_a\\otimes E\\)<\/span>. Changing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\)\">\\(a\\)<\/span> changes the particular range subspace while preserving its Hilbert dimension.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-WRC.md","start_offset":24694,"end_offset":26211,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ade7f2d077ad026b98a133fbcaf60d2fa99cefae5e4635c40d13805371d7e95a":{"markdown":"## The full fixed-point algebra\n\n**Theorem.** $N^\\theta=\\pi_\\alpha(M)$.\n\n**Proof.** Coefficients are fixed by (A3). For the reverse inclusion define on $\\mathcal H$\n\n<a id=\"equation-a11\"><\/a>\n\n$$[V_s\\xi](r)=U_s\\xi(r+s),\\qquad [W\\xi](r)=U_r\\xi(r).\n\\tag{A11}$$\n\nFor $W$, both this formula and the formula with $U_r^*$ preserve continuity and compact support of vector functions. Pointwise norm equality gives two inverse isometries on a dense class, hence inverse unitaries on $\\mathcal H$. No countable basis is chosen.\n\nEvery constant $b'\\in M'$ commutes with the regular generators. Also $V_s$ commutes with both families: for coefficients use $U_s\\alpha_{-(r+s)}(a)=\\alpha_{-r}(a)U_s$; for translations use commutativity of $G$. Thus all elements of $N$ commute with these operators.\n\nTake $x\\in N^\\theta$. It commutes with every $Q_\\chi$. The unitary $W$ commutes with $Q_\\chi$ and satisfies\n\n<a id=\"equation-a12\"><\/a>\n\n$$WV_sW^*=1_H\\otimes R_s,\\qquad R_s\\xi(r)=\\xi(r+s),\n\\tag{A12}$$\n\nbecause $U_rU_sU_{r+s}^*=1$. Hence $WxW^*$ commutes with $Q_\\chi$ and $R_s=L_{-s}$. Equation (A5) gives $WxW^*=a\\otimes1$ for some $a\\in B(H)$.\n\nWe still have to prove $a\\in M$. For fixed $b'\\in M'$, commutation of $x$ with $b'\\otimes1$ says that $a\\otimes1$ commutes with the multiplication operator $W(b'\\otimes1)W^*$, whose value is $U_r b'U_r^*$. For fixed $\\xi,\\eta\\in H$, the scalar function\n\n<a id=\"equation-a13\"><\/a>\n\n$$k(r)=\\langle[a,U_r b'U_r^*]\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n\\tag{A13}$$\n\nis continuous and bounded. Testing the zero operator between $h\\otimes\\xi$ and $g\\otimes\\eta$ gives $\\int h(r)\\overline{g(r)}k(r)\\,dr=0$ for every $h,g\\in C_c(G)$. Every compactly supported continuous test function occurs as $h\\overline g$, by choosing a cutoff $g=1$ on the support of $h$. Therefore $k=0$ everywhere: a nonzero value, rotated by a scalar phase, has positive real part in a neighborhood, and a nonnegative nonzero cutoff there would give a positive integral.\n\nIn particular $k(0)=0$ for every fixed $b',\\xi,\\eta$, so $[a,b']=0$ for every $b'\\in M'$. Thus $a\\in M''=M$ and\n\n<a id=\"equation-a14\"><\/a>\n\n$$x=W^*(a\\otimes1)W=\\pi_\\alpha(a).\n\\tag{A14}$$\n\nThe proof tests one continuous scalar function at a time; it does not intersect uncountably many conull sets. Normal regular-model comparison gives the assertion in every permitted model. $\\square$","html":"<h2>The full fixed-point algebra<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Theorem.<\/strong> <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N^\\theta=\\pi_\\alpha(M)\\)\">\\(N^\\theta=\\pi_\\alpha(M)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof.<\/strong> Coefficients are fixed by (A3). For the reverse inclusion define on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal H\\)\">\\(\\mathcal H\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[[V_s\\xi](r)=U_s\\xi(r+s),\\qquad [W\\xi](r)=U_r\\xi(r).\n\\tag{A11}\\]\">\\[[V_s\\xi](r)=U_s\\xi(r+s),\\qquad [W\\xi](r)=U_r\\xi(r).\n\\tag{A11}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span>, both this formula and the formula with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_r^*\\)\">\\(U_r^*\\)<\/span> preserve continuity and compact support of vector functions. Pointwise norm equality gives two inverse isometries on a dense class, hence inverse unitaries on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathcal H\\)\">\\(\\mathcal H\\)<\/span>. No countable basis is chosen.<\/p>\n<p>Every constant <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&#x27;\\in M&#x27;\\)\">\\(b&#x27;\\in M&#x27;\\)<\/span> commutes with the regular generators. Also <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_s\\)\">\\(V_s\\)<\/span> commutes with both families: for coefficients use <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_s\\alpha_{-(r+s)}(a)=\\alpha_{-r}(a)U_s\\)\">\\(U_s\\alpha_{-(r+s)}(a)=\\alpha_{-r}(a)U_s\\)<\/span>; for translations use commutativity of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>. Thus all elements of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span> commute with these operators.<\/p>\n<p>Take <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in N^\\theta\\)\">\\(x\\in N^\\theta\\)<\/span>. It commutes with every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q_\\chi\\)\">\\(Q_\\chi\\)<\/span>. The unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q_\\chi\\)\">\\(Q_\\chi\\)<\/span> and satisfies<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[WV_sW^*=1_H\\otimes R_s,\\qquad R_s\\xi(r)=\\xi(r+s),\n\\tag{A12}\\]\">\\[WV_sW^*=1_H\\otimes R_s,\\qquad R_s\\xi(r)=\\xi(r+s),\n\\tag{A12}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>because <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_rU_sU_{r+s}^*=1\\)\">\\(U_rU_sU_{r+s}^*=1\\)<\/span>. Hence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(WxW^*\\)\">\\(WxW^*\\)<\/span> commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q_\\chi\\)\">\\(Q_\\chi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(R_s=L_{-s}\\)\">\\(R_s=L_{-s}\\)<\/span>. Equation (A5) gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(WxW^*=a\\otimes1\\)\">\\(WxW^*=a\\otimes1\\)<\/span> for some <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in B(H)\\)\">\\(a\\in B(H)\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>We still have to prove <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M\\)\">\\(a\\in M\\)<\/span>. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&#x27;\\in M&#x27;\\)\">\\(b&#x27;\\in M&#x27;\\)<\/span>, commutation of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&#x27;\\otimes1\\)\">\\(b&#x27;\\otimes1\\)<\/span> says that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\otimes1\\)\">\\(a\\otimes1\\)<\/span> commutes with the multiplication operator <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W(b&#x27;\\otimes1)W^*\\)\">\\(W(b&#x27;\\otimes1)W^*\\)<\/span>, whose value is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U_r b&#x27;U_r^*\\)\">\\(U_r b&#x27;U_r^*\\)<\/span>. For fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\in H\\)<\/span>, the scalar function<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[k(r)=\\langle[a,U_r b&#x27;U_r^*]\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n\\tag{A13}\\]\">\\[k(r)=\\langle[a,U_r b&#x27;U_r^*]\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\n\\tag{A13}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>is continuous and bounded. Testing the zero operator between <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\otimes\\xi\\)\">\\(h\\otimes\\xi\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\otimes\\eta\\)\">\\(g\\otimes\\eta\\)<\/span> gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int h(r)\\overline{g(r)}k(r)\\,dr=0\\)\">\\(\\int h(r)\\overline{g(r)}k(r)\\,dr=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h,g\\in C_c(G)\\)\">\\(h,g\\in C_c(G)\\)<\/span>. Every compactly supported continuous test function occurs as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\overline g\\)\">\\(h\\overline g\\)<\/span>, by choosing a cutoff <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g=1\\)\">\\(g=1\\)<\/span> on the support of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\)\">\\(h\\)<\/span>. Therefore <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=0\\)\">\\(k=0\\)<\/span> everywhere: a nonzero value, rotated by a scalar phase, has positive real part in a neighborhood, and a nonnegative nonzero cutoff there would give a positive integral.<\/p>\n<p>In particular <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k(0)=0\\)\">\\(k(0)=0\\)<\/span> for every fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&#x27;,\\xi,\\eta\\)\">\\(b&#x27;,\\xi,\\eta\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\([a,b&#x27;]=0\\)\">\\([a,b&#x27;]=0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&#x27;\\in M&#x27;\\)\">\\(b&#x27;\\in M&#x27;\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\in M&#x27;&#x27;=M\\)\">\\(a\\in M&#x27;&#x27;=M\\)<\/span> and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[x=W^*(a\\otimes1)W=\\pi_\\alpha(a).\n\\tag{A14}\\]\">\\[x=W^*(a\\otimes1)W=\\pi_\\alpha(a).\n\\tag{A14}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The proof tests one continuous scalar function at a time; it does not intersect uncountably many conull sets. Normal regular-model comparison gives the assertion in every permitted model. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-DA.md","start_offset":8318,"end_offset":10652,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad30676ee050116d3fd3bdf19be9c9c5db77268c319a906b726c0af7623cf44a":{"markdown":"## The paired integral\n\nFor $f,g\\in\\mathcal K_\\alpha$, polarization of (A23) in the finite linear domain gives\n\n<a id=\"equation-a32\"><\/a>\n\n$$\\dot T_\\alpha(F(g)^*F(f))\n=\\pi_\\alpha\\left(\\int_G g(s)^*f(s)\\,ds\\right).\n\\tag{A32}$$\n\nIndeed multiply the squares for $f+i^k g$ by $i^k/4$ and sum for $k=0,1,2,3$. Only the cross term with $g^*$ on the left survives. All four squares have bounded weight values, so their linear combination lies in $\\mathfrak m_{T_\\alpha}$.\n\nFor $\\omega\\in N_*^+$ the scalar paired orbit is absolutely integrable, and\n\n<a id=\"equation-a33\"><\/a>\n\n$$\\int_{\\widehat G}\\omega(\\theta_\\chi(F(g)^*F(f)))\\,d\\chi\n=\\omega\\left(\\pi_\\alpha\\left(\\int_G g(s)^*f(s)\\,ds\\right)\\right).\n\\tag{A33}$$\n\nPointwise Cauchy–Schwarz for $\\omega$, then integral Cauchy–Schwarz and (A4), bounds the absolute integral by\n\n<a id=\"equation-a34\"><\/a>\n\n$$\\omega(T_\\alpha(F(g)^*F(g)))^{1/2}\n\\,\\omega(T_\\alpha(F(f)^*F(f)))^{1/2}<\\infty.\n\\tag{A34}$$\n\nPolarization proves (A33). General normal functionals are complex linear combinations of four positive normal functionals, so the assertion holds for them too. It makes no operator-norm Bochner integrability claim.","html":"<h2>The paired integral<\/h2>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f,g\\in\\mathcal K_\\alpha\\)\">\\(f,g\\in\\mathcal K_\\alpha\\)<\/span>, polarization of (A23) in the finite linear domain gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\\dot T_\\alpha(F(g)^*F(f))\n=\\pi_\\alpha\\left(\\int_G g(s)^*f(s)\\,ds\\right).\n\\tag{A32}\\]\">\\[\\dot T_\\alpha(F(g)^*F(f))\n=\\pi_\\alpha\\left(\\int_G g(s)^*f(s)\\,ds\\right).\n\\tag{A32}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Indeed multiply the squares for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(f+i^k g\\)\">\\(f+i^k g\\)<\/span> by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i^k/4\\)\">\\(i^k/4\\)<\/span> and sum for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(k=0,1,2,3\\)\">\\(k=0,1,2,3\\)<\/span>. Only the cross term with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g^*\\)\">\\(g^*\\)<\/span> on the left survives. All four squares have bounded weight values, so their linear combination lies in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathfrak m_{T_\\alpha}\\)\">\\(\\mathfrak m_{T_\\alpha}\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in N_*^+\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in N_*^+\\)<\/span> the scalar paired orbit is absolutely integrable, and<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\\int_{\\widehat G}\\omega(\\theta_\\chi(F(g)^*F(f)))\\,d\\chi\n=\\omega\\left(\\pi_\\alpha\\left(\\int_G g(s)^*f(s)\\,ds\\right)\\right).\n\\tag{A33}\\]\">\\[\\int_{\\widehat G}\\omega(\\theta_\\chi(F(g)^*F(f)))\\,d\\chi\n=\\omega\\left(\\pi_\\alpha\\left(\\int_G g(s)^*f(s)\\,ds\\right)\\right).\n\\tag{A33}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Pointwise Cauchy–Schwarz for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\)\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span>, then integral Cauchy–Schwarz and (A4), bounds the absolute integral by<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\\omega(T_\\alpha(F(g)^*F(g)))^{1/2}\n\\,\\omega(T_\\alpha(F(f)^*F(f)))^{1/2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\tag{A34}\\]\">\\[\\omega(T_\\alpha(F(g)^*F(g)))^{1/2}\n\\,\\omega(T_\\alpha(F(f)^*F(f)))^{1/2}&lt;\\infty.\n\\tag{A34}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Polarization proves (A33). General normal functionals are complex linear combinations of four positive normal functionals, so the assertion holds for them too. It makes no operator-norm Bochner integrability claim.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-DA.md","start_offset":19834,"end_offset":20988,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad7166855602717a3efde8d05f6566d7472eb597a2716c632be439e212e48c46":{"markdown":"## HC5. Source and retained work\n\nThe target is the homogeneous centralizer theorem in Takesaki, [*Theory of Operator Algebras II*](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4), Exercise XI.1.5, printed330–331. Its proposed route uses twisted two-by-two matrix fixed points, properly infinite projection comparison, a calculation with a unitary fiber generator, and stabilization under a sigma-finiteness hypothesis. Those mathematical antecedents are credited here.\n\nThe present proof is organized around the densely defined Gram isometry (HC4). It supplies scalar action on a fiber directly and does not consume those matrix-comparison or stabilization steps. Its arbitrary-algebra scope follows from that independently reviewed proof at its exact earlier inputs; it is not inferred from the source's restricted statement. Not all ancillary results of those historical works are reproved here.","html":"<h2>HC5. Source and retained work<\/h2>\n<p>The target is the homogeneous centralizer theorem in Takesaki, <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-10451-4\"><em>Theory of Operator Algebras II<\/em><\/a>, Exercise XI.1.5, printed330–331. Its proposed route uses twisted two-by-two matrix fixed points, properly infinite projection comparison, a calculation with a unitary fiber generator, and stabilization under a sigma-finiteness hypothesis. Those mathematical antecedents are credited here.<\/p>\n<p>The present proof is organized around the densely defined Gram isometry (HC4). It supplies scalar action on a fiber directly and does not consume those matrix-comparison or stabilization steps. Its arbitrary-algebra scope follows from that independently reviewed proof at its exact earlier inputs; it is not inferred from the source's restricted statement. Not all ancillary results of those historical works are reproved here.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-HCR.md","start_offset":13804,"end_offset":14697,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad9b035a92302c77060a30bf917139a80a8a713b39ff9723f74b8944ab2eadc4":{"markdown":"# Integrable actions: orbit averages, filters and actual eigenoperators\n\nAn integrable orbit has a weak Fourier field. The proof starts with the entire positive orbit average and its bounded domain, then proves a uniform coefficient estimate and the exact filter multiplier. Those steps identify the field support and show why an integrable action detects actual eigenoperators in every nonzero open localization.\n\nThe arbitrary LCH abelian group and arbitrary von Neumann algebra are retained. Weak C0 decay is distinguished from operator-norm decay. The real translation example is a separate exact model; it is not a countable reduction of the group.\n\n*Original expression and illustration are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held; earlier component terms remain their own.*","html":"<h1>Integrable actions: orbit averages, filters and actual eigenoperators<\/h1>\n<p>An integrable orbit has a weak Fourier field. The proof starts with the entire positive orbit average and its bounded domain, then proves a uniform coefficient estimate and the exact filter multiplier. Those steps identify the field support and show why an integrable action detects actual eigenoperators in every nonzero open localization.<\/p>\n<p>The arbitrary LCH abelian group and arbitrary von Neumann algebra are retained. Weak C0 decay is distinguished from operator-norm decay. The real translation example is a separate exact model; it is not a countable reduction of the group.<\/p>\n<p><em>Original expression and illustration are CC0-1.0 to the extent of rights held; earlier component terms remain their own.<\/em><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L114.md","start_offset":63,"end_offset":840,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad0b4e611d287f5dbb7ec43ca90d0ef511ba84cef35fc484728fd8efab95b724":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.intspace.dimone\"><\/a>\n## One-dimensional internal spaces are unitary lines\n\n**Unitary-line theorem.** Let $K=\\mathbb Cx$ be one dimensional and internal.  Normalize $x$ in its\nHilbert norm and write $u=x/\\|x\\|_K$.  Equation (U1) gives\n\n$$\nu^*u=1,\n\\tag{U7}\n$$\n\nso $u$ is an isometry.  The projection $1-uu^*$ annihilates $u$:\n\n$$\n(1-uu^*)u=0.\n\\tag{U8}\n$$\n\nIt therefore annihilates all of $K$, and fullness forces $uu^*=1$.  Hence\n$u$ is unitary and $K=\\mathbb Cu$.\n\nConversely, if $u\\in\\mathcal U(M)$, then $\\mathbb Cu$ satisfies (U1), is\ncomplete, and is full: $au=0$ implies $a=0$.  Thus\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\dim K=1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\mathbb Cu\\text{ for some }u\\in\\mathcal U(M).}\n\\tag{U9}\n$$","html":"<h2>One-dimensional internal spaces are unitary lines<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Unitary-line theorem.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\mathbb Cx\\)\">\\(K=\\mathbb Cx\\)<\/span> be one dimensional and internal.  Normalize <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\)\">\\(x\\)<\/span> in its\nHilbert norm and write <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u=x/\\|x\\|_K\\)\">\\(u=x/\\|x\\|_K\\)<\/span>.  Equation (U1) gives<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\nu^*u=1,\n\\tag{U7}\n\\]\">\\[\nu^*u=1,\n\\tag{U7}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> is an isometry.  The projection <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1-uu^*\\)\">\\(1-uu^*\\)<\/span> annihilates <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span>:<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n(1-uu^*)u=0.\n\\tag{U8}\n\\]\">\\[\n(1-uu^*)u=0.\n\\tag{U8}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<p>It therefore annihilates all of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K\\)\">\\(K\\)<\/span>, and fullness forces <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(uu^*=1\\)\">\\(uu^*=1\\)<\/span>.  Hence\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> is unitary and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(K=\\mathbb Cu\\)\">\\(K=\\mathbb Cu\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Conversely, if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)\">\\(u\\in\\mathcal U(M)\\)<\/span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb Cu\\)\">\\(\\mathbb Cu\\)<\/span> satisfies (U1), is\ncomplete, and is full: <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(au=0\\)\">\\(au=0\\)<\/span> implies <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=0\\)\">\\(a=0\\)<\/span>.  Thus<\/p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n\\boxed{\\dim K=1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\mathbb Cu\\text{ for some }u\\in\\mathcal U(M).}\n\\tag{U9}\n\\]\">\\[\n\\boxed{\\dim K=1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nK=\\mathbb Cu\\text{ for some }u\\in\\mathcal U(M).}\n\\tag{U9}\n\\]<\/span><\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-L130.md","start_offset":17826,"end_offset":18540,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad6165089eb3717b82f201e186fed632e00c8705400e3c46a24554ee0956348d":{"markdown":"<a id=\"oa-flow.msf.static\"><\/a>\n## 2. The static interface is already proved\n\n**Proposition 2.1.** Let \\(M\\ne0\\) have separable predual. Fix any standard form \\((M,H,J,P)\\) and a unital von Neumann subalgebra \\(D\\subseteq Z(M)\\). There is a standard probability space and a measurable field of nonzero separable standard forms \\((M_x,H_x,J_x,P_x)\\) with compatible identifications\n\\[\n D=L^\\infty(X,\\mu),\\quad H=\\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad M=\\int_X^\\oplus M_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad J=\\int_X^\\oplus J_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad P=\\{\\xi\\in H:\\xi_x\\in P_x\\text{ a.e.}\\}.\n \\tag{MSF1}\n\\]\nThe algebra equality contains every essentially bounded measurable section in the fibre algebras. There are countable measurable contraction sections strong-star dense in \\((M_x)_1\\), and countable measurable vector sections norm-dense in \\(P_x\\), or in its unit part. If \\(D=Z(M)\\), the fibres are factors almost everywhere. The probability may be replaced by any equivalent sigma-finite measure, with the corresponding unitary change of Hilbert coordinates.\n\n**Proof by exact existing-provider specialization.** Apply L41's construction with the one-element locally compact group and its trivial action. Its continuity, separability and invariance hypotheses then hold for every specified \\(D\\). The necessary separability of the chosen standard Hilbert space and existence of a faithful normal state are proved at L41 lines 89–102, using CR's cone-vector norm estimate and spanning theorem. Thus no separably acting representation or cyclic separating vector is assumed without proof.\n\nThe complete algebra disintegration is [DC7](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#existence-over-any-specified-central-abelian-subalgebra), applied explicitly at L41 lines 164–166. [MW7](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-MW.html#central-disintegration-of-a-separable-weighted-algebra) gives the compatible field of actual GNS spaces and full left/right involution graphs; this is stronger than decomposition of selected algebra generators. L41 lines 168–187 normalize the fibre weights by\n\\[\n r(x)=\\widetilde\\varphi_x(1),\\qquad\n d\\mu=r\\,d\\widetilde\\mu,\n \\qquad \\varphi_x=r^{-1}\\widetilde\\varphi_x.\n \\tag{MSF2}\n\\]\nThe all-positive MW formula at \\(1\\) proves \\(0<r<\\infty\\) almost everywhere and \\(\\int r\\,d\\widetilde\\mu=1\\). Its compatible GNS transport has fibre norm factor \\(r^{-1/2}\\), compensated by the change of measure. It is a unitary of integrals; the coordinate maps are not incorrectly treated as fibre unitaries. The base identification at L41 lines 189–210 is a proved Borel isomorphism on conull sets. L41 (A16), lines 211–224, proves the Hilbert, whole-algebra, GNS and modular identities, including all closed-operator domains.\n\nThe cone identity is proved in [L41's cone construction](OA-FLOW-L41.md#oa-flow.cstd.cone), lines 233–287. There the [GFR7](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-GFR.html#a-common-countable-strong-dense-contraction-family) contractions \\(q_i(x)\\) give positive sections \\(a_j=kq_i^*q_i\\), and\n\\[\n v_j(x)=\\Delta_x^{1/4}a_j(x)\\Omega_x,\n \\qquad \\Omega_x=\\Lambda_{\\varphi_x}(1).\n \\tag{MSF3}\n\\]\nThe proof explicitly verifies that \\(a_j\\Omega_x\\) lies in the half-power domain and bounds the quarter power by both vector and adjoint-vector norms. Bounded spectral truncations prove measurability. This is not an assumption that unbounded modular powers act on arbitrary measurable sections. The \\(v_j(x)\\) are norm-dense in \\(P_x\\). Finite measurable pastes of their positive preimages approximate any square-integrable cone section, with squared error less than \\(2n^{-2}\\); this proves exactly the last equality in (MSF1). Radial truncation supplies dense cone-unit-ball sections. Consequently all clauses of the proposition except factoriality and the optional measure change are literal conclusions of the cited completed proof.\n\nFor \\(D=Z(M)\\), apply [DC8](../../OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#centers-and-the-factor-decomposition), lines 231–253, to that same full algebra field. Its centre formula and countable non-scalar-centre tests prove that nonfactor fibres form a null set. Remove this and the construction's other Borel null exceptions. L41's defaults on its discarded set may be nonfactors when \\(M\\) is not a factor; they are not used to claim factoriality everywhere. The restricted conull static model has nonzero factor fibres at every retained point.\n\nFor \\(d\\nu=s\\,d\\mu\\), with \\(0<s<\\infty\\) almost everywhere, the map\n\\[\n H_\\mu\\longrightarrow H_\\nu,\\qquad\n \\xi(x)\\longmapsto s(x)^{-1/2}\\xi(x)\n \\tag{MSF4}\n\\]\nis onto isometric by integration; its inverse uses \\(s^{1/2}\\). Positive scalar multiplication preserves each cone, and commutes with its antiunitary conjugation because the scalar is real. Algebra sections are unchanged. Equivalent sigma-finite measures have such a positive finite Radon–Nikodym density. This proves the optional change and preserves every clause. \\(\\square\\)\n\nThe construction therefore provides the specified compatible algebra and standard-form fields without an additional central-reduction assumption.","html":"<h2>2. The static interface is already proved<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Proposition 2.1.<\/strong> Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\ne0\\)\">\\(M\\ne0\\)<\/span> have separable predual. Fix any standard form <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((M,H,J,P)\\)\">\\((M,H,J,P)\\)<\/span> and a unital von Neumann subalgebra <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\subseteq Z(M)\\)\">\\(D\\subseteq Z(M)\\)<\/span>. There is a standard probability space and a measurable field of nonzero separable standard forms <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((M_x,H_x,J_x,P_x)\\)\">\\((M_x,H_x,J_x,P_x)\\)<\/span> with compatible identifications\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n D=L^\\infty(X,\\mu),\\quad H=\\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad M=\\int_X^\\oplus M_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad J=\\int_X^\\oplus J_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad P=\\{\\xi\\in H:\\xi_x\\in P_x\\text{ a.e.}\\}.\n \\tag{MSF1}\n\\]\">\\[\n D=L^\\infty(X,\\mu),\\quad H=\\int_X^\\oplus H_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad M=\\int_X^\\oplus M_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad J=\\int_X^\\oplus J_x\\,d\\mu,\n \\quad P=\\{\\xi\\in H:\\xi_x\\in P_x\\text{ a.e.}\\}.\n \\tag{MSF1}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe algebra equality contains every essentially bounded measurable section in the fibre algebras. There are countable measurable contraction sections strong-star dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\((M_x)_1\\)\">\\((M_x)_1\\)<\/span>, and countable measurable vector sections norm-dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_x\\)\">\\(P_x\\)<\/span>, or in its unit part. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D=Z(M)\\)\">\\(D=Z(M)\\)<\/span>, the fibres are factors almost everywhere. The probability may be replaced by any equivalent sigma-finite measure, with the corresponding unitary change of Hilbert coordinates.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Proof by exact existing-provider specialization.<\/strong> Apply L41's construction with the one-element locally compact group and its trivial action. Its continuity, separability and invariance hypotheses then hold for every specified <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D\\)\">\\(D\\)<\/span>. The necessary separability of the chosen standard Hilbert space and existence of a faithful normal state are proved at L41 lines 89–102, using CR's cone-vector norm estimate and spanning theorem. Thus no separably acting representation or cyclic separating vector is assumed without proof.<\/p>\n<p>The complete algebra disintegration is <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#existence-over-any-specified-central-abelian-subalgebra\">DC7<\/a>, applied explicitly at L41 lines 164–166. <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-MW.html#central-disintegration-of-a-separable-weighted-algebra\">MW7<\/a> gives the compatible field of actual GNS spaces and full left/right involution graphs; this is stronger than decomposition of selected algebra generators. L41 lines 168–187 normalize the fibre weights by\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n r(x)=\\widetilde\\varphi_x(1),\\qquad\n d\\mu=r\\,d\\widetilde\\mu,\n \\qquad \\varphi_x=r^{-1}\\widetilde\\varphi_x.\n \\tag{MSF2}\n\\]\">\\[\n r(x)=\\widetilde\\varphi_x(1),\\qquad\n d\\mu=r\\,d\\widetilde\\mu,\n \\qquad \\varphi_x=r^{-1}\\widetilde\\varphi_x.\n \\tag{MSF2}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe all-positive MW formula at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(1\\)\">\\(1\\)<\/span> proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;r&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(0&lt;r&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span> almost everywhere and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\int r\\,d\\widetilde\\mu=1\\)\">\\(\\int r\\,d\\widetilde\\mu=1\\)<\/span>. Its compatible GNS transport has fibre norm factor <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(r^{-1/2}\\)\">\\(r^{-1/2}\\)<\/span>, compensated by the change of measure. It is a unitary of integrals; the coordinate maps are not incorrectly treated as fibre unitaries. The base identification at L41 lines 189–210 is a proved Borel isomorphism on conull sets. L41 (A16), lines 211–224, proves the Hilbert, whole-algebra, GNS and modular identities, including all closed-operator domains.<\/p>\n<p>The cone identity is proved in <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-L41.html#oa-flow.cstd.cone\">L41's cone construction<\/a>, lines 233–287. There the <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-GFR.html#a-common-countable-strong-dense-contraction-family\">GFR7<\/a> contractions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(q_i(x)\\)\">\\(q_i(x)\\)<\/span> give positive sections <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_j=kq_i^*q_i\\)\">\\(a_j=kq_i^*q_i\\)<\/span>, and\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n v_j(x)=\\Delta_x^{1/4}a_j(x)\\Omega_x,\n \\qquad \\Omega_x=\\Lambda_{\\varphi_x}(1).\n \\tag{MSF3}\n\\]\">\\[\n v_j(x)=\\Delta_x^{1/4}a_j(x)\\Omega_x,\n \\qquad \\Omega_x=\\Lambda_{\\varphi_x}(1).\n \\tag{MSF3}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe proof explicitly verifies that <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a_j\\Omega_x\\)\">\\(a_j\\Omega_x\\)<\/span> lies in the half-power domain and bounds the quarter power by both vector and adjoint-vector norms. Bounded spectral truncations prove measurability. This is not an assumption that unbounded modular powers act on arbitrary measurable sections. The <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v_j(x)\\)\">\\(v_j(x)\\)<\/span> are norm-dense in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P_x\\)\">\\(P_x\\)<\/span>. Finite measurable pastes of their positive preimages approximate any square-integrable cone section, with squared error less than <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(2n^{-2}\\)\">\\(2n^{-2}\\)<\/span>; this proves exactly the last equality in (MSF1). Radial truncation supplies dense cone-unit-ball sections. Consequently all clauses of the proposition except factoriality and the optional measure change are literal conclusions of the cited completed proof.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(D=Z(M)\\)\">\\(D=Z(M)\\)<\/span>, apply <a href=\"../courses/OA-MOD/OA-MOD-DC.html#centers-and-the-factor-decomposition\">DC8<\/a>, lines 231–253, to that same full algebra field. Its centre formula and countable non-scalar-centre tests prove that nonfactor fibres form a null set. Remove this and the construction's other Borel null exceptions. L41's defaults on its discarded set may be nonfactors when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M\\)\">\\(M\\)<\/span> is not a factor; they are not used to claim factoriality everywhere. The restricted conull static model has nonzero factor fibres at every retained point.<\/p>\n<p>For <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(d\\nu=s\\,d\\mu\\)\">\\(d\\nu=s\\,d\\mu\\)<\/span>, with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(0&lt;s&lt;\\infty\\)\">\\(0&lt;s&lt;\\infty\\)<\/span> almost everywhere, the map\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n H_\\mu\\longrightarrow H_\\nu,\\qquad\n \\xi(x)\\longmapsto s(x)^{-1/2}\\xi(x)\n \\tag{MSF4}\n\\]\">\\[\n H_\\mu\\longrightarrow H_\\nu,\\qquad\n \\xi(x)\\longmapsto s(x)^{-1/2}\\xi(x)\n \\tag{MSF4}\n\\]<\/span>\nis onto isometric by integration; its inverse uses <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(s^{1/2}\\)\">\\(s^{1/2}\\)<\/span>. Positive scalar multiplication preserves each cone, and commutes with its antiunitary conjugation because the scalar is real. Algebra sections are unchanged. Equivalent sigma-finite measures have such a positive finite Radon–Nikodym density. This proves the optional change and preserves every clause. <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\square\\)\">\\(\\square\\)<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The construction therefore provides the specified compatible algebra and standard-form fields without an additional central-reduction assumption.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-MSF.md","start_offset":3829,"end_offset":8830,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad6780e0d378a3cff40f785fc3651c935b37d123f4ba81581ec2a22145b094ed":{"markdown":"<a id=\"dd-recognition\"><\/a>\n## 4. The particular infinite periodic weight gives its own decomposition\n\nLet \\(\\phi\\) be the given infinite periodic weight of Section 2. On \\(M_2(M)\\), use\n\\(\\Psi(X)=\\phi(X_{11})+\\lambda\\phi(X_{22})\\).\n[BC3](OA-FLOW-BC.md#oa-flow.bc.3)–[BC4](OA-FLOW-BC.md#oa-flow.bc.4)'s full balanced modular formula gives\n\\[\n \\sigma_t^\\Psi(X)=\n \\begin{pmatrix}\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{11})&\\lambda^{-it}\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{12})\\\\\n \\lambda^{it}\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{21})&\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{22})\n \\end{pmatrix}.\n \\tag{DDP14}\n\\]\nIt has period \\(P\\). Formula (DDP13) supplies a fixed \\(v=u e_{21}\\) with \\(u\\) unitary and \\(\\sigma_t^\\phi(u)=\\lambda^{-it}u\\). Set \\(U=u^*\\). The fixed unitary \\(v+v^*\\), together with [CZ0's whole-cone invariance](OA-FLOW-CZ.md#oa-flow.cz.0), gives\n\\[\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(U)=\\lambda^{it}U,\\qquad\n \\phi(UxU^*)=\\lambda\\phi(x)\\quad(x\\in M_+).\n \\tag{DDP15}\n\\]\nThis whole-cone argument does not require \\(U\\) to have a finite GNS vector.\n\nPut \\(N=M_\\phi\\), \\(\\theta=\\operatorname{Ad}U|_N\\), \\(\\tau=\\phi|_N\\). Then \\(\\tau\\theta=\\lambda\\tau\\) on the entire cone. The degree-\\(n\\) space is \\(NU^n=U^nN\\), by multiplying an eigenoperator by \\(U^{-n}\\). [PF1](OA-FLOW-PF.md#oa-flow.pf.1)'s bounded strong-star Fejér sums give \\(M=(N\\cup\\{U\\})''\\).\n\nThe full normal recognition is more than this generation statement. Define\n\\[\n W(\\delta_n\\otimes\\Lambda_\\tau(a))=\\Lambda_\\phi(U^na)\n \\quad(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau).\n \\tag{DDP16}\n\\]\nEquation \\(\\phi=\\tau E\\) makes distinct degrees orthogonal and makes each diagonal inner product \\(\\tau(b^*a)\\), so \\(W\\) is isometric. Choose finite-trace positive contractions \\(c_i\\uparrow1\\) in \\(N\\). [CZ0](OA-FLOW-CZ.md#oa-flow.cz.0)'s exact right-multiplier formula gives \\(\\Lambda_\\phi(xc_i)\\to\\Lambda_\\phi(x)\\) for \\(x\\in\\mathfrak n_\\phi\\). At fixed \\(i\\), the bounded Fejér approximants \\(T_m(x)\\) satisfy\n\\(\\Lambda_\\phi(T_m(x)c_i)\\to\\Lambda_\\phi(xc_i)\\), since this is bounded strong convergence tested on \\(\\Lambda_\\phi(c_i)\\). Each approximant is a finite sum of vectors in (DDP16): \\(U^{-n}P_n(x)c_i\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau\\). The range of \\(W\\) is closed and contains the dense whole GNS range, so \\(W\\) is onto.\n\nIts conjugation formulas are\n\\[\n (W^*\\pi_\\phi(d)W\\xi)_n\n       =\\pi_\\tau(\\theta^{-n}(d))\\xi_n,\\qquad\n (W^*\\pi_\\phi(U)W\\xi)_n=\\xi_{n-1}.\n \\tag{DDP17}\n\\]\nThey hold first on the displayed dense vectors, then everywhere by boundedness. Hence conjugation by \\(W\\) and the faithful normal GNS representation give\n\\[\n N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb Z\\ \\cong\\ M,\\qquad d\\mapsto d,\\quad s\\mapsto U,\n \\tag{DDP18}\n\\]\non the entire von Neumann algebras, with normal inverse, exactly as [GT4](OA-FLOW-GT.md#oa-flow.gt.4)–[GT5](OA-FLOW-GT.md#oa-flow.gt.5). The non-countable finite-trace approximants are a net; the Fourier index remains \\(\\mathbb Z\\). This proves existence and recognition for the specified weight at arbitrary factor cardinality.\n\nIf a finite centralizer corner is normalized as \\(\\bar\\phi=\\phi(e)^{-1}\\phi|_{eMe}\\), its trace amplification transports as \\(\\phi(e)(\\bar\\phi\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr})\\). The scalar \\(\\phi(e)\\) cannot be silently removed. The balanced proof above requires no claim that independently chosen amplifications preserve the two weights.","html":"<h2>4. The particular infinite periodic weight gives its own decomposition<\/h2>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi\\)\">\\(\\phi\\)<\/span> be the given infinite periodic weight of Section 2. On <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M_2(M)\\)\">\\(M_2(M)\\)<\/span>, use\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Psi(X)=\\phi(X_{11})+\\lambda\\phi(X_{22})\\)\">\\(\\Psi(X)=\\phi(X_{11})+\\lambda\\phi(X_{22})\\)<\/span>.\n<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#oa-flow.bc.3\">BC3<\/a>–<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-BC.html#oa-flow.bc.4\">BC4<\/a>'s full balanced modular formula gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sigma_t^\\Psi(X)=\n \\begin{pmatrix}\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{11})&amp;\\lambda^{-it}\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{12})\\\\\n \\lambda^{it}\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{21})&amp;\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{22})\n \\end{pmatrix}.\n \\tag{DDP14}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sigma_t^\\Psi(X)=\n \\begin{pmatrix}\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{11})&amp;\\lambda^{-it}\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{12})\\\\\n \\lambda^{it}\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{21})&amp;\\sigma_t^\\phi(X_{22})\n \\end{pmatrix}.\n \\tag{DDP14}\n\\]<\/span>\nIt has period <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span>. Formula (DDP13) supplies a fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v=u e_{21}\\)\">\\(v=u e_{21}\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\)\">\\(u\\)<\/span> unitary and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\sigma_t^\\phi(u)=\\lambda^{-it}u\\)\">\\(\\sigma_t^\\phi(u)=\\lambda^{-it}u\\)<\/span>. Set <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U=u^*\\)\">\\(U=u^*\\)<\/span>. The fixed unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(v+v^*\\)\">\\(v+v^*\\)<\/span>, together with <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CZ.html#oa-flow.cz.0\">CZ0's whole-cone invariance<\/a>, gives\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(U)=\\lambda^{it}U,\\qquad\n \\phi(UxU^*)=\\lambda\\phi(x)\\quad(x\\in M_+).\n \\tag{DDP15}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\sigma_t^\\phi(U)=\\lambda^{it}U,\\qquad\n \\phi(UxU^*)=\\lambda\\phi(x)\\quad(x\\in M_+).\n \\tag{DDP15}\n\\]<\/span>\nThis whole-cone argument does not require <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U\\)\">\\(U\\)<\/span> to have a finite GNS vector.<\/p>\n<p>Put <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N=M_\\phi\\)\">\\(N=M_\\phi\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\theta=\\operatorname{Ad}U|_N\\)\">\\(\\theta=\\operatorname{Ad}U|_N\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau=\\phi|_N\\)\">\\(\\tau=\\phi|_N\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau\\theta=\\lambda\\tau\\)\">\\(\\tau\\theta=\\lambda\\tau\\)<\/span> on the entire cone. The degree-<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> space is <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(NU^n=U^nN\\)\">\\(NU^n=U^nN\\)<\/span>, by multiplying an eigenoperator by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U^{-n}\\)\">\\(U^{-n}\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-PF.html#oa-flow.pf.1\">PF1<\/a>'s bounded strong-star Fejér sums give <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(M=(N\\cup\\{U\\})&#x27;&#x27;\\)\">\\(M=(N\\cup\\{U\\})&#x27;&#x27;\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The full normal recognition is more than this generation statement. Define\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n W(\\delta_n\\otimes\\Lambda_\\tau(a))=\\Lambda_\\phi(U^na)\n \\quad(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau).\n \\tag{DDP16}\n\\]\">\\[\n W(\\delta_n\\otimes\\Lambda_\\tau(a))=\\Lambda_\\phi(U^na)\n \\quad(a\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau).\n \\tag{DDP16}\n\\]<\/span>\nEquation <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi=\\tau E\\)\">\\(\\phi=\\tau E\\)<\/span> makes distinct degrees orthogonal and makes each diagonal inner product <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\tau(b^*a)\\)\">\\(\\tau(b^*a)\\)<\/span>, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is isometric. Choose finite-trace positive contractions <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(c_i\\uparrow1\\)\">\\(c_i\\uparrow1\\)<\/span> in <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(N\\)\">\\(N\\)<\/span>. <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CZ.html#oa-flow.cz.0\">CZ0<\/a>'s exact right-multiplier formula gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\phi(xc_i)\\to\\Lambda_\\phi(x)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\phi(xc_i)\\to\\Lambda_\\phi(x)\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x\\in\\mathfrak n_\\phi\\)\">\\(x\\in\\mathfrak n_\\phi\\)<\/span>. At fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(i\\)\">\\(i\\)<\/span>, the bounded Fejér approximants <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(T_m(x)\\)\">\\(T_m(x)\\)<\/span> satisfy\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\phi(T_m(x)c_i)\\to\\Lambda_\\phi(xc_i)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\phi(T_m(x)c_i)\\to\\Lambda_\\phi(xc_i)\\)<\/span>, since this is bounded strong convergence tested on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Lambda_\\phi(c_i)\\)\">\\(\\Lambda_\\phi(c_i)\\)<\/span>. Each approximant is a finite sum of vectors in (DDP16): <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(U^{-n}P_n(x)c_i\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau\\)\">\\(U^{-n}P_n(x)c_i\\in\\mathfrak n_\\tau\\)<\/span>. The range of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is closed and contains the dense whole GNS range, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> is onto.<\/p>\n<p>Its conjugation formulas are\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n (W^*\\pi_\\phi(d)W\\xi)_n\n       =\\pi_\\tau(\\theta^{-n}(d))\\xi_n,\\qquad\n (W^*\\pi_\\phi(U)W\\xi)_n=\\xi_{n-1}.\n \\tag{DDP17}\n\\]\">\\[\n (W^*\\pi_\\phi(d)W\\xi)_n\n       =\\pi_\\tau(\\theta^{-n}(d))\\xi_n,\\qquad\n (W^*\\pi_\\phi(U)W\\xi)_n=\\xi_{n-1}.\n \\tag{DDP17}\n\\]<\/span>\nThey hold first on the displayed dense vectors, then everywhere by boundedness. Hence conjugation by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(W\\)\">\\(W\\)<\/span> and the faithful normal GNS representation give\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb Z\\ \\cong\\ M,\\qquad d\\mapsto d,\\quad s\\mapsto U,\n \\tag{DDP18}\n\\]\">\\[\n N\\rtimes_\\theta\\mathbb Z\\ \\cong\\ M,\\qquad d\\mapsto d,\\quad s\\mapsto U,\n \\tag{DDP18}\n\\]<\/span>\non the entire von Neumann algebras, with normal inverse, exactly as <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GT.html#oa-flow.gt.4\">GT4<\/a>–<a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-GT.html#oa-flow.gt.5\">GT5<\/a>. The non-countable finite-trace approximants are a net; the Fourier index remains <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\mathbb Z\\)\">\\(\\mathbb Z\\)<\/span>. This proves existence and recognition for the specified weight at arbitrary factor cardinality.<\/p>\n<p>If a finite centralizer corner is normalized as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\bar\\phi=\\phi(e)^{-1}\\phi|_{eMe}\\)\">\\(\\bar\\phi=\\phi(e)^{-1}\\phi|_{eMe}\\)<\/span>, its trace amplification transports as <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi(e)(\\bar\\phi\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr})\\)\">\\(\\phi(e)(\\bar\\phi\\otimes\\operatorname{Tr})\\)<\/span>. The scalar <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\phi(e)\\)\">\\(\\phi(e)\\)<\/span> cannot be silently removed. The balanced proof above requires no claim that independently chosen amplifications preserve the two weights.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-DDP.md","start_offset":16914,"end_offset":20138,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"},"ad192eb92bce711a236701d7d9415c534fb704127798429ecfb2a9d12faa6aa2":{"markdown":"<a id=\"nb-tails\"><\/a>\n## 5. The action and an exact noncentral-triviality witness\n\nThe permutation of tensor sites \\(h\\mapsto gh\\) defines a unitary \\(V_g\\) on \\(H\\), fixing \\(\\Omega\\). It gives \\(V_gV_k=V_{gk}\\), commutes with \\(J\\) and \\(\\Delta\\), and implements (NB2). Thus \\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}(V_g)|_P\\) is a normal, state-preserving action, and it preserves \\(Q\\).\n\nEnumerate pairwise distinct sites \\(h_n\\). They eventually escape every finite subset of \\(G\\). Let \\(z_n\\) be \\(z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\) at \\(h_n\\), and the identity elsewhere. Then \\(z_n=z_n^*=z_n^{-1}\\) and \\(z_n\\in Q\\). Since \\(gh_n\\ne h_n\\) for \\(g\\ne e\\), product independence gives, for every \\(n\\),\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n m&=\\varphi(z_n)=\\frac{\\lambda-1}{\\lambda+1},\\\\\n \\|\\beta_g(z_n)-z_n\\|_\\varphi^2\n   &=2-2m^2=\\frac{8\\lambda}{(1+\\lambda)^2}>0\n       \\qquad(g\\ne e).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NB9}\n\\]\nThe state is nontracial, so \\(m\\) is not zero. The adjoint seminorm has the same value because this difference is selfadjoint.\n\nWe check the definition involving the **whole predual**. Call a bounded sequence \\(x_n\\) centralizing when \\(\\|x_n\\omega-\\omega x_n\\|\\to0\\) for every \\(\\omega\\in P_*\\), where the two module functionals evaluate \\(Y\\mapsto\\omega(Yx_n)\\) and \\(Y\\mapsto\\omega(x_nY)\\). If \\(\\xi=a\\Omega,\\eta=b\\Omega\\) with \\(a,b\\) local and \\(h_n\\) outside both supports, left multiplication by \\(z_n\\) agrees on \\(\\xi,\\eta\\) with right multiplication by \\(z_n\\). This follows from \\(z\\rho^{1/2}=\\rho^{1/2}z\\). The right operator is selfadjoint and commutes with every \\(Y\\in P\\). Moving it across the coefficient pairing shows\n\\(\\langle Yz_n\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle z_nY\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)\nfor all \\(Y\\), so the functional commutator is exactly zero for such a pair.\n\nThe complete [CP-01–06 predual proof](../../OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6) says that every normal functional is a restricted square-summable vector series and that its predual norm is its functional norm. Truncate such a series; Cauchy-Schwarz bounds the tail norm. Approximate its finitely many vectors by local \\(a\\Omega,b\\Omega\\); the elementary bound \\(\\|\\omega_{\\xi,\\eta}-\\omega_{\\xi',\\eta'}\\|\\le\\|\\xi-\\xi'\\|\\|\\eta\\|+\\|\\xi'\\|\\|\\eta-\\eta'\\|\\) bounds the error. Commutation by a norm-one \\(z_n\\) changes that error by at most a factor two. The preceding exact vanishing then proves \\(\\|z_n\\omega-\\omega z_n\\|\\to0\\) for every \\(\\omega\\in P_*\\). Thus \\(z_n\\) is centralizing at the precise predual definition.\n\nIt also asymptotically commutes strongly-star with every fixed \\(u\\in P\\). Indeed \\(z_n\\) is in the functional centralizer, so right multiplication by \\(z_n\\) preserves \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_\\varphi\\). For a local \\(a=E_F(u)\\) and \\(h_n\\notin F\\),\n\\[\n \\|[u,z_n]\\|_\\varphi\\le2\\|u-a\\|_\\varphi.\n \\tag{NB10}\n\\]\nFirst make the right side small by increasing \\(F\\), then let \\(n\\) increase. Apply the argument to \\(u^*\\) for the adjoints. A uniformly bounded sequence \\(Y_n\\in P\\) with \\(Y_n\\Omega\\to0\\) converges strongly to zero: on each local right vector \\(b'\\Omega\\), \\(Y_nb'\\Omega=b'Y_n\\Omega\\to0\\), and those vectors are dense. This proves the claimed strong-star convergence, not just one vector estimate.\n\nAn automorphism is centrally trivial if its displacement tends strongly-star to zero on every bounded predual-centralizing sequence. Equation (NB9) contradicts that requirement for every \\(g\\ne e\\), whereas the identity plainly has it. It also proves outerness directly: if \\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}(u)\\), then\n\\(\\|\\beta_g(z_n)-z_n\\|_\\varphi=\\|[u,z_n]u^*\\|_\\varphi\\to0\\)\nby strong convergence from (NB10), tested at the fixed vector \\(u^*\\Omega\\). This contradicts (NB9). More strongly, no unitary of \\(P\\) can implement \\(\\beta_g\\) **on \\(Q\\)**, because all the \\(z_n\\) belong to \\(Q\\). This stronger conclusion will remove every nonidentity Fourier coefficient below.","html":"<h2>5. The action and an exact noncentral-triviality witness<\/h2>\n<p>The permutation of tensor sites <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h\\mapsto gh\\)\">\\(h\\mapsto gh\\)<\/span> defines a unitary <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_g\\)\">\\(V_g\\)<\/span> on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(H\\)\">\\(H\\)<\/span>, fixing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\Omega\\)<\/span>. It gives <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(V_gV_k=V_{gk}\\)\">\\(V_gV_k=V_{gk}\\)<\/span>, commutes with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(J\\)\">\\(J\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\Delta\\)\">\\(\\Delta\\)<\/span>, and implements (NB2). Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}(V_g)|_P\\)\">\\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}(V_g)|_P\\)<\/span> is a normal, state-preserving action, and it preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Enumerate pairwise distinct sites <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_n\\)\">\\(h_n\\)<\/span>. They eventually escape every finite subset of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(G\\)\">\\(G\\)<\/span>. Let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span> be <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\)\">\\(z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\)<\/span> at <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_n\\)\">\\(h_n\\)<\/span>, and the identity elsewhere. Then <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n=z_n^*=z_n^{-1}\\)\">\\(z_n=z_n^*=z_n^{-1}\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\in Q\\)\">\\(z_n\\in Q\\)<\/span>. Since <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(gh_n\\ne h_n\\)\">\\(gh_n\\ne h_n\\)<\/span> for <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\ne e\\)\">\\(g\\ne e\\)<\/span>, product independence gives, for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n m&amp;=\\varphi(z_n)=\\frac{\\lambda-1}{\\lambda+1},\\\\\n \\|\\beta_g(z_n)-z_n\\|_\\varphi^2\n   &amp;=2-2m^2=\\frac{8\\lambda}{(1+\\lambda)^2}&gt;0\n       \\qquad(g\\ne e).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NB9}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n m&amp;=\\varphi(z_n)=\\frac{\\lambda-1}{\\lambda+1},\\\\\n \\|\\beta_g(z_n)-z_n\\|_\\varphi^2\n   &amp;=2-2m^2=\\frac{8\\lambda}{(1+\\lambda)^2}&gt;0\n       \\qquad(g\\ne e).\n \\end{aligned}\n \\tag{NB9}\n\\]<\/span>\nThe state is nontracial, so <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(m\\)\">\\(m\\)<\/span> is not zero. The adjoint seminorm has the same value because this difference is selfadjoint.<\/p>\n<p>We check the definition involving the <strong>whole predual<\/strong>. Call a bounded sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(x_n\\)\">\\(x_n\\)<\/span> centralizing when <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|x_n\\omega-\\omega x_n\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|x_n\\omega-\\omega x_n\\|\\to0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in P_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in P_*\\)<\/span>, where the two module functionals evaluate <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\mapsto\\omega(Yx_n)\\)\">\\(Y\\mapsto\\omega(Yx_n)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\mapsto\\omega(x_nY)\\)\">\\(Y\\mapsto\\omega(x_nY)\\)<\/span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi=a\\Omega,\\eta=b\\Omega\\)\">\\(\\xi=a\\Omega,\\eta=b\\Omega\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a,b\\)\">\\(a,b\\)<\/span> local and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_n\\)\">\\(h_n\\)<\/span> outside both supports, left multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span> agrees on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)\">\\(\\xi,\\eta\\)<\/span> with right multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span>. This follows from <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z\\rho^{1/2}=\\rho^{1/2}z\\)\">\\(z\\rho^{1/2}=\\rho^{1/2}z\\)<\/span>. The right operator is selfadjoint and commutes with every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\in P\\)\">\\(Y\\in P\\)<\/span>. Moving it across the coefficient pairing shows\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\langle Yz_n\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle z_nY\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)\">\\(\\langle Yz_n\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle z_nY\\xi,\\eta\\rangle\\)<\/span>\nfor all <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y\\)\">\\(Y\\)<\/span>, so the functional commutator is exactly zero for such a pair.<\/p>\n<p>The complete <a href=\"../courses/OA-FLOW/OA-FLOW-CP.html#oa-flow.cp.6\">CP-01–06 predual proof<\/a> says that every normal functional is a restricted square-summable vector series and that its predual norm is its functional norm. Truncate such a series; Cauchy-Schwarz bounds the tail norm. Approximate its finitely many vectors by local <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a\\Omega,b\\Omega\\)\">\\(a\\Omega,b\\Omega\\)<\/span>; the elementary bound <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\omega_{\\xi,\\eta}-\\omega_{\\xi&#x27;,\\eta&#x27;}\\|\\le\\|\\xi-\\xi&#x27;\\|\\|\\eta\\|+\\|\\xi&#x27;\\|\\|\\eta-\\eta&#x27;\\|\\)\">\\(\\|\\omega_{\\xi,\\eta}-\\omega_{\\xi&#x27;,\\eta&#x27;}\\|\\le\\|\\xi-\\xi&#x27;\\|\\|\\eta\\|+\\|\\xi&#x27;\\|\\|\\eta-\\eta&#x27;\\|\\)<\/span> bounds the error. Commutation by a norm-one <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span> changes that error by at most a factor two. The preceding exact vanishing then proves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|z_n\\omega-\\omega z_n\\|\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|z_n\\omega-\\omega z_n\\|\\to0\\)<\/span> for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\omega\\in P_*\\)\">\\(\\omega\\in P_*\\)<\/span>. Thus <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span> is centralizing at the precise predual definition.<\/p>\n<p>It also asymptotically commutes strongly-star with every fixed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u\\in P\\)\">\\(u\\in P\\)<\/span>. Indeed <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span> is in the functional centralizer, so right multiplication by <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span> preserves <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_\\varphi\\)\">\\(\\|\\cdot\\|_\\varphi\\)<\/span>. For a local <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(a=E_F(u)\\)\">\\(a=E_F(u)\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(h_n\\notin F\\)\">\\(h_n\\notin F\\)<\/span>,\n<span class=\"math display\" data-tex=\"\\[\n \\|[u,z_n]\\|_\\varphi\\le2\\|u-a\\|_\\varphi.\n \\tag{NB10}\n\\]\">\\[\n \\|[u,z_n]\\|_\\varphi\\le2\\|u-a\\|_\\varphi.\n \\tag{NB10}\n\\]<\/span>\nFirst make the right side small by increasing <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(F\\)\">\\(F\\)<\/span>, then let <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(n\\)\">\\(n\\)<\/span> increase. Apply the argument to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*\\)\">\\(u^*\\)<\/span> for the adjoints. A uniformly bounded sequence <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_n\\in P\\)\">\\(Y_n\\in P\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_n\\Omega\\to0\\)\">\\(Y_n\\Omega\\to0\\)<\/span> converges strongly to zero: on each local right vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(b&#x27;\\Omega\\)\">\\(b&#x27;\\Omega\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Y_nb&#x27;\\Omega=b&#x27;Y_n\\Omega\\to0\\)\">\\(Y_nb&#x27;\\Omega=b&#x27;Y_n\\Omega\\to0\\)<\/span>, and those vectors are dense. This proves the claimed strong-star convergence, not just one vector estimate.<\/p>\n<p>An automorphism is centrally trivial if its displacement tends strongly-star to zero on every bounded predual-centralizing sequence. Equation (NB9) contradicts that requirement for every <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(g\\ne e\\)\">\\(g\\ne e\\)<\/span>, whereas the identity plainly has it. It also proves outerness directly: if <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}(u)\\)\">\\(\\beta_g=\\operatorname{Ad}(u)\\)<\/span>, then\n<span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\|\\beta_g(z_n)-z_n\\|_\\varphi=\\|[u,z_n]u^*\\|_\\varphi\\to0\\)\">\\(\\|\\beta_g(z_n)-z_n\\|_\\varphi=\\|[u,z_n]u^*\\|_\\varphi\\to0\\)<\/span>\nby strong convergence from (NB10), tested at the fixed vector <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(u^*\\Omega\\)\">\\(u^*\\Omega\\)<\/span>. This contradicts (NB9). More strongly, no unitary of <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(P\\)\">\\(P\\)<\/span> can implement <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(\\beta_g\\)\">\\(\\beta_g\\)<\/span> <strong>on <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span><\/strong>, because all the <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(z_n\\)\">\\(z_n\\)<\/span> belong to <span class=\"math\" data-tex=\"\\(Q\\)\">\\(Q\\)<\/span>. This stronger conclusion will remove every nonidentity Fourier coefficient below.<\/p>\n","source":"docs/courses/OA-FLOW/src/OA-FLOW-NB.md","start_offset":14032,"end_offset":17882,"role":"registered_statement_and_proof_passage"}});
